  "details": "When browsing the Worlds, Social, or Avatar tabs, there can often be a noticeable frame drop or \"lag spike\" as the client attempts to fetch and render many thumbnails simultaneously. \n\nI suggest implementing BlurHash for these UI elements. By storing a short hash string (12-30 characters) alongside image metadata, the client can immediately render a colorful, blurred placeholder locally while the full image loads in the background with a lower priority. \n\nBenefits:\n\nSmoother UI Performance: Reduces the hitching caused by loading many raw images at once.\n\nBetter UX: Replaces empty boxes or generic loading icons with a visually pleasing representation of the content.\n\nLow Overhead: The hashes are small enough to be included in existing JSON responses without significantly increasing data usage.\n\nThis would make navigating the main menus feel much more fluid, especially on lower-end hardware or slower connections.",
